A||||8||Phantom Rage||DIS Coils||||
Z||000000||Phantom Rage||08-03-99||06:42 PM||||Hello all, I am new to your group, but I have been monitoring the forum for a while.  I know most of you aren't into supping up the duke.  This is for the few who do, however this message is only for Dis 87 and 88 dukes.<br>This is probally old news to you guys but here goes. Accel now makes these cool looking "accel yellow" Dis coils.  The part number I found on there web page under www.mrgasket.com, in the new products section, is 140017.  To be honest I'm not sure if these will work, but it states that they were designed for AC/Delco style DIS systems, from 86-Present.  I will call or email them to confirm this, but from the pictures they kinda look the same.  Please check the web site for your self though.  They have a comparison table with there coil vs.OE.  Well good luck all.<br>P.S. I am very,very serous about turbocharging the duke.  I have almost everything I need except a Boost timming master control. I contacted MSD, the tech said there DIS-2 system is timing dependent not boost, but they are working on a BTM, which should be ready next year.  That's a long wait.  So if anyone out there knows of a better BTM system which will work for DIS please notify me, thx.   
Z||000001||Phantom Rage||08-03-99||07:04 PM||||Opps, silly me, I meant Msd's Dis-2 system is RPM dependent not timming.  
Z||000002||theogre||08-03-99||07:38 PM||||I thought DIS-2 was new, but I'm told it's been out for over a year.<p>The coils I'll have to look at. After so many years, I just want them replaced for reliability.<p>There is a definate limit to the options available for the 87& up Iron Duke. In addition to DIS, the TBI unit, and ECM, are also compleat redesigns.
Z||000003||Ozzy||09-29-1999||07:31 PM||||Hey Phantom...let me know the progress of that project. I also wan't to turbo my "Duke" but have met with a lack of interest here. (If I can ever get it to run reliably that is!)<P>My e-mail is valid and would appreciate any info on the turbo system you assemble to do the job.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||09-29-1999||08:01 PM||||I would like to have more power from my duck, but any major modifications seem to really shorten the life expectancy of the motor, so I just drive the car and appreciate it for what it is- one of the greatest cars ever built, just not the fastest. Good luck on your turbo project, it sounds like fun.||||reg||6
Z||000005||ChadMan||09-30-1999||10:02 AM||||Have any of you read the Motor Trend article from 1984 on a company called Pfaff that made turbos for Iron Duke Fieros?  The article is in Brooklands' Fiero book.<P>Anyway, the Pfaff kit provided mild boost (something like 8 psi) and made around 140 hp.  It was about as quick as the V6 Fieros turned out to be.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Phantom Rage||10-02-1999||05:30 PM||||Finally after a month I just recieved my Accel DiS coils from Summit($67.38), the guy gave me a better deal, forgot the going price.  I just installed them.  I had to make a minor mod to the ignition module.  Two plastic tabs had to be grinded of with a dremel for the coils to fit flush on the module shield.  Was there a difference? No not really, just a slightly better Idle.    I might try a slightly larger gap, perhaps then I will get a bit of power.  I know these ignition upgrades usually don't do a thing unless your original equiptment is bad to start with, especially since the engine never see's over 5g rpm.  I guess mine were fine.  <BR>   I will post more about the turbo project later in the future, for now I am trying to pass my classes.||||reg||12
Z||000007||theogre||10-02-1999||08:52 PM||||Do Not over gap plugs on a DIS system. Even with the Accell coils. These coils are built for an OEM gap. Increasing the gap is likely to damage the coils.<P>Coil boosting generally only improves performance in high compression engines. Why? very simple,,, the higher the compression the more energy is needed to create a spark.<P>If that's all you cashed out for them it's still a good deal. Borg Warner ones at Pep Boys are nearly that. Nearly all the replacement DIS coils I've seen are 25-30$us each. Since Accell is making these to work with All GM DIS systems then the quality is likely a bit better. Since Accell is known for high performance parts, these coils are also built to work properly even on compression boosted motors. That means they should last the rest of the life of your car. (I've had one Accell SS coil on 3 cars now..... It's nearly 20 years old. I think I'll put it on my caravan. might help its cold blooded behavior.)||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phantom Rage||10-03-1999||07:40 PM||||Hey thx Orge, I'll take your advise.  I just remembered increasing the gap on my Dodge Daytona, and it did make a slight difference, but I had a MSD system on it.  Thx again. ||||reg||12
